Key Points to Remember

. Exception handling helps your program deal with unexpected errors (like missing files or read/write problems)
without crashing.
. Always use a try-catch block when working with files.
. Catch specific exceptions first - like FileNotFoundException before a general IOException .
. Use the finally block (or try-with-resources ) to close the file and release resources - even if something goes
wrong.
. Show clear and friendly messages to the user when an error occurs.





Key Points to Remember

. BufferedReader and BufferedWriter are used to read/write text efficiently in Java.
. They are built on top of FileReader/FileWriter, providing buffering to improve performance.
. BufferedReader reads one line at a time using .readLine() .
. BufferedWriter writes text using .write() and moves to the next line using .newLine() .
. Using a buffer means Java doesn't access the file system every time-it works with chunks of data in memory.
. Try-with-resources automatically handles closing and flushing streams safely.
. flush() is used to manually push any remaining buffered data to the file before closing.
. Buffered streams are best for working with large text files or when reading/writing many lines.
